      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Etretinate, a neutral lipophilic retinoid ester, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 40%, supporting meaningful absorption for lipophilic retinoid derivatives.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Some reports describe retinoic acid as having low and variable absolute bioavailability due to gut degradation, but this is not threshold-specific and is outweighed by quantitative retinoid bioavailability data above 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is fenretinide, a retinoid amide of 4-aminophenol. Although no direct fenretinide bioavailability record is present, the closest retinoid analogs—tretinoin, isotretinoin, acitretin, and etretinate—consistently show oral bioavailability values of about 40–60%, all above the 20% threshold. The amide modification and lipophilicity may reduce absorption relative to the acids, but no affirmative evidence supports a fall below 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for fenretinide itself is provided.",
    "The effect of replacing the retinoid carboxylate with a neutral p-hydroxyphenyl amide on human intestinal absorption is not directly quantified in the evidence.",
